sh and sh2 are equal on the domain of sh (proved by SAT: 1314 AIG nodes, 1327 conflicts)
> s1(x: i32) = { s: i32 = 0; for i in 0..3 { s += x << i }; s }
> s3(x: i32) = (x << 4) - x
> equiv s1 s3
s1 and s3 are equal on the domain of s1 (proved by SAT: 1208 AIG nodes, 8389 conflicts)
> t1(x: u32, y: u32) = [3, 5, x, y][x & 3]
> t2(x: u32, y: u32) = (x & 3) == 0 ? 3 : (x & 3) == 1 ? 5 : (x & 3) == 2 ? x : y
> equiv t1 t2
t1 and t2 are equal on the domain of t1 (proved by SAT: 531 AIG nodes, 126 conflicts)
> m1(x: u32, y: u32) = x * y
> m2(x: u32, y: u32) = y * x
> equiv m1 m2
